T37.01|ESG integration considers financially relevant environmental, social and governance factors alongside other investment information. It differs from excluding sectors, pursuing social impact or simply holding a high-scoring label. Relevance depends on the company, horizon and investment thesis.~~Good sustainability characteristics do not justify any purchase price. Evaluate how a factor changes cash flows, risks or capital requirements.|Integration: Incorporates relevant factors into analysis.;;Screening: Includes or excludes defined exposures.;;Thematic/impact: Targets a theme or measurable outcome.|A factory faces an assumed ₹5 lakh annual compliance cost not included in a valuation forecast.~~Identify the affected cash flows.;;Model costs and possible investment responses.;;Reassess value rather than merely changing an ESG score.~~Integration connects evidence with economics.~~Applicable obligations and estimates require local verification.|Identify financially relevant factors.;;Connect them to cash and risk.;;Keep price discipline and clear objectives.
T37.02|Sustainability reporting communicates relevant risks, opportunities and impacts under the applicable framework. Financial materiality considers information relevant to investor decisions; impact materiality considers effects on people or the environment under frameworks using that lens. Their scope should not be assumed identical.~~Metrics need boundaries, methods and assurance context. Different reporting standards and local implementation dates require current primary sources.|Financial materiality: Relevance to financial prospects and investor decisions.;;Impact materiality: Significance of outward effects under the framework.;;Reporting boundary: Entities and activities included.|A company reports emissions down 20% after selling a high-emission subsidiary.~~Check whether the reporting boundary changed.;;Compare like-for-like operations where possible.;;Separate disposal effects from operational improvements.~~A reported reduction need not imply an equivalent system-wide reduction.~~Reporting requirements depend on the framework.|State framework and boundary.;;Reconcile changes and methods.;;Review assurance and data limitations.
T37.03|Physical climate risk arises from acute events and chronic changes; transition risk from policy, technology, market and legal changes toward lower emissions. Both can affect assets, operations, insurance and funding. Exposure varies by location and business model.~~Scenario analysis explores possibilities rather than predicting a single climate path. Include adaptation costs and counterparty or supply-chain dependencies.|Acute physical: Floods, storms and similar events.;;Chronic physical: Long-run heat, water or other changes.;;Transition: Changes in policy, demand and technology.|A coastal facility earns ₹10 lakh annual cash but a stress scenario assumes ₹20 repair cost and one year interruption.~~Model repair spending separately.;;Remove the affected year's cash.;;Assess insurance, adaptation and future exposure.~~Damage and interruption create distinct cash effects.~~Probabilities and local conditions require specialist evidence.|Map assets and supply chains.;;Use coherent scenarios.;;Assess adaptation, insurance and financing.
T37.04|Green bonds generally link proceeds to eligible green projects under a framework; sustainability-linked bonds connect financial or structural terms to specified issuer performance targets. A label alone does not guarantee additional impact, strong targets or low credit risk.~~Review use-of-proceeds tracking or KPI definitions, verification and consequences. Credit analysis remains necessary because repayment rights and sustainability objectives are separate questions.|Use-of-proceeds bond: Funds specified eligible activities.;;Sustainability-linked bond: Terms respond to defined performance.;;Verification: External review of relevant claims or results.|A fictional bond has ₹100 lakh principal and a 0.25-point coupon step-up if an emissions target is missed.~~Annual extra interest is ₹25,000.;;Compare that consequence with target ambition and business scale.;;Assess issuer credit separately.~~A small penalty may provide limited economic incentive.~~Actual instruments require their complete terms.|Read framework and KPIs.;;Assess ambition and verification.;;Keep credit risk separate from the label.
T37.05|Carbon pricing assigns a cost to specified emissions through taxes, trading systems or other mechanisms. Carbon credits and allowances represent different rights or claims and should not be treated interchangeably. Quality depends on measurement, additionality, permanence and double-counting controls where relevant.~~A purchased offset does not physically remove the company's own emissions. Analyse gross emissions and claimed compensation separately.|Carbon tax: Charge per qualifying emissions unit.;;Allowance: Permission under a capped system.;;Credit: Claimed reduction or removal under a programme.|A facility emits 1,000 tonnes and a hypothetical carbon charge is ₹2,000 per tonne.~~Direct annual charge is ₹20 lakh.;;A project cutting emissions 200 tonnes saves ₹4 lakh at that price.;;Compare savings with project cost and other benefits.~~Carbon price can change investment economics.~~No current market price or regulatory eligibility is asserted.|Verify scheme and units.;;Assess credit quality and double counting.;;Stress prices and operational responses.
T37.06|Impact investing seeks intentional positive outcomes alongside financial objectives, with measurement of contribution. Outputs count activity; outcomes describe changes; impact asks what happened because of the intervention relative to an appropriate counterfactual. These distinctions prevent exaggerated claims.~~Financial return and social effect should each be assessed explicitly. A positive outcome may have occurred without the investment.|Output: Immediate activity or delivery.;;Outcome: Change experienced by beneficiaries.;;Additional impact: Change attributable to the intervention.|A programme funds 100 training places, 80 completions and 40 jobs; a comparable untreated group suggests 25 jobs might have occurred anyway.~~Record outputs and completions separately.;;Illustrative incremental employment is 15.;;Investigate comparability before attributing causality.~~Gross outcomes are not automatically additional impact.~~A valid counterfactual requires rigorous design.|Define intended outcomes.;;Use credible comparison evidence.;;Report financial and impact results transparently.
T37.07|Blended finance combines concessional or development resources with commercial capital to support eligible projects. Guarantees, junior capital or grants can alter risk allocation and mobilise investment. Public support should address a justified market failure rather than merely subsidise an otherwise viable deal.~~Assess additionality, cost and who receives benefits. Private leverage ratios alone do not establish development value.|Concessional capital: Funding on preferential terms.;;Guarantee: Shares defined loss risk.;;Technical assistance: Supports capacity or preparation.|A project uses ₹20 lakh concessional junior capital and ₹80 commercial senior capital.~~Total financing is ₹100.;;A first ₹15 loss is absorbed by junior capital under the assumed structure.;;Assess public benefit and whether support was necessary.~~Blending reallocates risk rather than eliminating it.~~Actual loss priority follows the documents.|Explain the market failure.;;Model risk allocation.;;Measure additionality and public cost.
T37.08|Financial inclusion concerns access to useful, affordable and responsible financial services. Opening an account is an input, not proof of meaningful use or improved wellbeing. Costs, trust, accessibility and consumer protection affect outcomes.~~Digital access can widen reach while excluding people without devices, literacy or reliable connectivity. Credit access should be judged with repayment burden and suitability.|Access: Ability to obtain services.;;Usage: Effective and sustained participation.;;Quality/outcome: Affordability, protection and useful results.|A programme opens 1,000 accounts but only 200 have recurring useful activity.~~Reported access count is 1,000.;;Active-use measure is 20% under the definition.;;Investigate cost, usability and customer needs.~~Account numbers alone overstate effective inclusion.~~Low activity can have several explanations, not only failure.|Measure access and usage separately.;;Assess affordability and harm.;;Design for actual customer constraints.
T37.09|Stewardship uses ownership rights, engagement and voting to influence company conduct and protect long-term value. Engagement needs clear objectives and escalation, while divestment changes ownership rather than directly stopping an activity. Collaborative efforts can increase influence but require appropriate governance.~~Track outcomes and avoid claiming causality from every change after a meeting. Voting records should align with stated priorities.|Engagement: Dialogue with explicit objectives.;;Voting: Uses formal shareholder decisions.;;Escalation: Changes approach when progress is inadequate.|An investor asks for clearer capital-allocation disclosure and sets a twelve-month review.~~Define measurable requested changes.;;Record meetings and voting decisions.;;Compare actual disclosure and decisions with the objective.~~Activity is not the same as achieved influence.~~Other stakeholders may contribute to the result.|Set objectives and review dates.;;Align voting with priorities.;;Report outcomes and limitations.
T37.10|Greenwashing overstates or misrepresents sustainability attributes. ESG data limitations include inconsistent boundaries, estimated values, missing observations and divergent scoring methods. A high rating can reflect relative performance under one methodology rather than low absolute impact.~~Inspect underlying facts and definitions. Do not average incompatible scores and treat the result as objective truth.|Claim risk: Marketing exceeds supported evidence.;;Measurement risk: Inputs are incomplete or inconsistent.;;Methodology risk: Scores weight factors differently.|Two rating providers give the same firm 80 and 40 out of 100.~~Compare included dimensions and weights.;;Review whether one measures financial risk and the other impacts.;;Inspect source data before combining scores.~~Different scores can answer different questions.~~Disagreement alone does not prove either is wrong.|Verify claims against data.;;Read methods and boundaries.;;Disclose estimation and missingness.
T37.11|Islamic finance applies Shariah principles to financial arrangements, including restrictions on riba, excessive uncertainty and prohibited activities, with emphasis on valid contractual structures. Compliance interpretation and governance require qualified review. Economic risk does not disappear because a structure is compliant.~~Compare the actual asset, profit-sharing and payment arrangements rather than substituting conventional labels. Jurisdiction and Shariah-board decisions affect implementation.|Asset-based trade: Financing through valid purchase and sale.;;Leasing: Payment for use of an asset.;;Partnership: Shares business outcomes under agreed terms.|An institution buys an asset for ₹100 lakh and sells it at a disclosed ₹110 deferred price under an assumed valid sale arrangement.~~Identify actual purchase and ownership steps.;;Review delivery, payment and risk allocation.;;Seek qualified compliance review of the contract.~~Contract form and execution matter.~~This example is introductory, not a Shariah ruling.|Understand the real transaction.;;Review risks and ownership.;;Use qualified legal and Shariah guidance.
T37.12|Murabaha is a disclosed cost-plus sale; ijara leases asset use; musharaka is a capital partnership; mudaraba combines capital provision with management under defined profit-sharing and loss rules; sukuk represent specified rights under their structure. These are distinct contracts, not interchangeable product names.~~Return and loss allocation depend on valid terms and conduct. Sukuk can have different asset rights, recourse and credit exposure.|Murabaha/ijara: Sale financing and leasing.;;Musharaka/mudaraba: Partnership and investment-management arrangements.;;Sukuk: Certificates representing defined contractual rights.|Assume a partnership earns ₹20 lakh distributable profit with agreed 60/40 profit shares.~~Allocate ₹12 and ₹8.;;Review capital contributions and contractual loss rules separately.;;Do not assume profit percentages automatically determine all losses.~~Profit and loss allocation can follow different principles.~~Detailed validity needs qualified review.|Identify the contract type.;;Read profit, loss and recourse terms.;;Verify compliance and enforceable asset rights.
T37.13|Cooperative and community finance organise resources around member or community needs. Governance, member rights and surplus allocation can differ from investor-owned institutions. Social purpose does not remove credit, liquidity or operational risks.~~Transparent decision rules and sustainable economics protect both mission and resources. Informal trust should be supported by clear records and safeguards.|Cooperative: Member-owned financial arrangement.;;Community lending: Supports local needs under a defined model.;;Mutual support: Shares resources and risks among participants.|A member pool collects ₹10 lakh, lends ₹8 and keeps ₹2 liquid; members request ₹3 withdrawals.~~Immediate liquid shortfall is ₹1.;;Review loan maturities and withdrawal rules.;;Avoid relying on social purpose to resolve timing.~~Mission and liquidity management are both necessary.~~Legal permissions and protections vary.|Clarify member rights.;;Maintain reliable records and controls.;;Stress withdrawals and loan losses.
T37.14|Nonprofit and endowment finance manages resources to sustain a mission. Restricted gifts may only fund specified purposes, while unrestricted funds support broader operations. Endowment spending policies balance current service with future purchasing power under investment uncertainty.~~A large reported asset balance can coexist with operating shortages when funds are restricted or illiquid. Mission objectives should be connected to a cash and investment policy.|Restricted funds: Donor or legal limits on use.;;Unrestricted funds: Available within broader governance rules.;;Endowment: Capital managed for ongoing support.|A nonprofit has ₹100 lakh assets, but ₹80 is restricted and next-year general operating need is ₹25.~~Unrestricted resources are ₹20 before other constraints.;;General funding gap is ₹5.;;Review permissible income and fundraising options.~~Total assets overstate freely usable resources.~~Restrictions must be read from governing documents.|Track restrictions separately.;;Link spending to sustainable resources.;;Plan liquidity and governance for the mission.
